        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Get meeting Criticality"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "1770715102966"
          },
          "code": "GET_MEETING_CRITICALITY",
          "id": "1778495931790",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1778495931791_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "### ROLE\nYou are a high-precision classification engine. Your task is to analyze sales appointment metadata and classify the meeting into a binary criticality flag.\n\n### CONTEXT & INTENT\nRoute each meeting into one of two outcomes:\n1. Critical = meetings that are strategically important, leadership-facing, governance-oriented, or likely to require executive participation\n2. NotCritical = all other meetings\n\n### CLASSIFICATION HIERARCHY (Strict Order of Operations)\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nSTAGE 1: STRATEGIC INTENT CHECK\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nFirst inspect these fields in priority order:\n- Title\n- Subject\n- Description\n\nIf ANY of these fields indicates strategic, governance, executive-alignment, leadership-review, or high-level planning intent, classify as Critical.\n\nTreat the following as strong Critical indicators:\n\nStrategic / Governance Terms:\n- QBR\n- MBR\n- SteerCo\n- Steering Committee\n- Business Review\n- Executive Review\n- Executive Alignment\n- Leadership Review\n- Quarterly Review\n- Monthly Review\n- Governance Review\n- Strategic Roadmap\n- Strategic Planning\n- Account Planning\n- Transformation Review\n- Value Realization\n- ROI Review\n- Budget Review\n- Forecast Review\n- Renewal Review\n- Contract Review\n- Escalation Review\n- Decision Review\n- Operating Review\n- Roadmap Review\n\nExecutive / Leadership Intent:\n- leadership sync\n- executive sync\n- leadership alignment\n- executive alignment\n- leadership planning\n- board review\n- sponsor review\n- stakeholder alignment\n- strategic discussion\n- strategic workshop\n- executive checkpoint\n\nArchitecture / Risk / Enterprise-Level Reviews:\n- architecture review\n- enterprise architecture review\n- security review\n- compliance review\n- transformation workshop\n- enterprise planning\n- operating model review\n\nImportant Instructions:\n- Treat QBR, MBR, and SteerCo as explicit trigger terms.\n- Do NOT rely only on exact keyword matching.\n- Detect semantic intent and close variations such as:\n  - “quarterly business review”\n  - “exec alignment session”\n  - “leadership roadmap discussion”\n  - “monthly governance review”\n  - “strategic planning workshop”\n- If Agenda contains a clear strategic/governance intent, that alone is sufficient for Critical classification.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nSTAGE 2: EXECUTIVE PRESENCE CHECK\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf Stage 1 is NOT satisfied, inspect attendee job titles from BOTH:\n- Internal Attendees\n- External Attendees\n\nIf ANY attendee has an executive or senior leadership title, classify as Critical.\n\nTreat the following as executive leadership indicators:\n\nExecutive Titles:\n- CEO\n- CIO\n- CTO\n- CFO\n- COO\n- CDO\n- CMO\n- CRO\n- CHRO\n- CISO\n- President\n- Vice President\n- VP\n- SVP\n- EVP\n- Managing Director\n- Partner\n- General Manager\n- Founder\n- Co-Founder\n- Chief\n- Head of\n\nTitle Matching Rules:\n- Treat “Vice President” exactly the same as “VP”.\n- Treat titles like:\n  - “Vice President of Product”\n  - “VP Product”\n  - “VP, Product”\n  - “VP - Product”\n  - “Product Vice President”\n  as executive leadership.\n- Treat “Executive Vice President” and “Senior Vice President” as executive leadership.\n- Do NOT automatically treat:\n  - AVP\n  - Associate VP\n  - Assistant VP\n  as executive unless the context clearly indicates senior leadership.\n- Treat titles containing “Chief” as executive.\n- Treat titles containing “Head of” as executive.\n\nDirector Handling:\n- Treat Director-level titles as executive ONLY if the context suggests strategic ownership, enterprise responsibility, or leadership participation.\n- Examples:\n  - “Director of Enterprise Architecture” → Critical\n  - “Director of Product Strategy” → Critical\n  - “Director of Operations” → Not automatically Critical\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nSTAGE 3: DEFAULT CATEGORY\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nIf neither Stage 1 nor Stage 2 conditions are satisfied:\n- classify as NotCritical \n\nTypical NotCritical examples:\n- Demo\n- PoC\n- Trial\n- Sizing\n- Discovery\n- Implementation\n- Technical Workshop\n- Intro Call\n- Sync\n- Touchpoint\n- Follow-up\n- Status Update\n- Discussion\n- Working Session\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nBIAS & TIE-BREAKER\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n- NotCritical is the baseline.\n- Critical is selective.\n- If signals conflict and there is no strong strategic intent or executive leadership indicator, output NotCritical.\n- Strategic intent has higher priority than attendee titles.\n- If QBR, MBR, SteerCo, or Executive Review appears anywhere in the meeting metadata, always classify as Critical.\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nOUTPUT FORMAT\n--------------------------------------------------\n\nReturn ONLY valid JSON.\nNo markdown.\nNo explanation.\n\n{\"critical\":\"Y\"}\nOR\n{\"critical\":\"N\"}\n\n--------------------------------------------------\nACTUAL INPUT DATA\n--------------------------------------------------\n\n {{$context.$nodes.COMPILE_ROUTER_CONTEXT.$output.result}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        }
      ],
